  data: {
    LLM: 'Game: Primiera. Logic: See rules.objective and rules.gameplay.',
    Player: "Primiera (often confused with the related Spanish 'Primero') is a prestigious 16th-century Italian card game that is mathematically and strategically recognized as a direct ancestor of modern Poker. It is a betting and card-comparing game where players aim to form combinations from a 4-card hand. Known for its unique 'Primiera' combination (one card of each suit) and its high-stakes 'Vying' (betting) system, it was a game of status and intellect across Renaissance Europe.",
    objective: 'Win the pot by having the highest combination or forcing others to fold.',
    gameplay: 'All players ante. Each receives four cards dealt two at a time from a 40-card Italian deck. Betting (vying) follows: players may match, raise, or fold; dealer bets first; play is counter-clockwise. A player may knock (Vada) for immediate showdown. Best hand wins the pot. Hand ranks: Chorus (four of a kind) highest, then Fluxus (flush), Supremus, Primiera (one of each suit), Numerus (point total in one suit). Card point values: 7=21, 6=18, Ace=16, 5=15, 4=14, 3=13, 2=12, face=10.',
    keyRules: [
      '40-card deck; four cards each dealt two at a time.',
      'All ante before deal. Vying: match, raise, or fold; dealer bets first; counter-clockwise.',
      'Hand ranks: Chorus (four of a kind) > Fluxus (flush) > Supremus > Primiera (one per suit) > Numerus (point).',
      'Point values: 7=21, 6=18, A=16, 5=15, 4=14, 3=13, 2=12, face=10.',
      'Knock (Vada) forces immediate showdown. Best hand wins pot.',
